--TEST--
A user's own secret lives in their session sealed under a key ring, and it survives `regenerate` because what it is bound to is the application and the name rather than the identifier — `rule:http-server/a-session-holds-a-secret-only-sealed`, `rule:http-server/session-expiry-belongs-to-the-store`
--SKIPIF--
<?nvs
// The record crosses a real store, which is the point: `setSecret` is worth
// asserting because what leaves this process is ciphertext. `[cache.shared]`
// below names `tests/db/compose.yaml`'s `redis`, and a tree without one skips
// rather than reporting the store's absence as a failure of the sealed door.
//
// The store is asked directly rather than through `Core\Session::start`, which
// refuses where no request arrived
// (`rule:security/request-state-throws-in-an-isolate`) — and this scaffolding
// answers none whatever the case does, since a `--GET--` section describes the
// program under `--FILE--` alone (`crates/nvs-test`'s module doc).
try {
    Core\Cache::shared()->get("whether-a-session-case-has-a-store-to-cross");
} catch (Throwable $nowhere) {
    echo "skip - ", $nowhere->message, "\n";
}
--GET--
under=a-request
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A refresh token is a user's own secret and has to outlive the request that
// received it. A cache tier may evict at any time, so storing one there logs a
// user out on a footprint decision; the session is where it goes, and it goes
// there sealed, so that what the store holds is ciphertext.
secret bytes $key = Core\Crypto::generateKey();
array<secret bytes> $ring = [$key];
secret string $token = "sk-live-7";

Core\Session::start();
Core\Session::setSecret("refresh", $token, $ring);

echo "the sealed door reads back what it wrote: ",
    (Core\Session::getSecret("refresh", $ring) ?? "") == $token ? "yes" : "no", "\n";

// `regenerate` issues a new identifier and moves the record to it, which is
// what a login does the moment it hardens. A value bound to the old identifier
// would stop opening at exactly that moment, so it is bound to the application
// and the entry's name instead.
Core\Session::regenerate();
echo "and again after the identifier changed under it: ",
    (Core\Session::getSecret("refresh", $ring) ?? "") == $token ? "yes" : "no", "\n";

// The bound asserted on the other side, so the two lines above are the seal
// and not a member that hands back whatever it is asked for: a ring that
// sealed nothing here opens nothing, and it is a miss rather than an error
// because a rotated ring is meant to re-fetch rather than fail a request.
secret bytes $theirs = Core\Crypto::generateKey();
array<secret bytes> $other = [$theirs];
echo "a ring that sealed nothing here is a miss: ",
    Core\Session::getSecret("refresh", $other) == null ? "null" : "something", "\n";

// And the name is sealed in as well as looked up, so one ring does not read a
// secret under a second name.
echo "a name nothing sealed under is a miss: ",
    Core\Session::getSecret("never-written", $ring) == null ? "null" : "something", "\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
# The store a session record has to live in for a request on another core to
# find it, and the grant over it. `redis` is `tests/db/compose.yaml`'s service,
# on the loopback port that file publishes it under.
[[app]]
entry = "case.nvs"

[app.capabilities.cache]
shared = true

[session]
backend = "shared"

[cache.shared]
url = "redis://127.0.0.1:16379"
--EXPECT--
the sealed door reads back what it wrote: yes
and again after the identifier changed under it: yes
a ring that sealed nothing here is a miss: null
a name nothing sealed under is a miss: null
